  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1231 AM MDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nMonday and Tuesday...Upper trough over the Pacific NW opens up, and \nthe upper low center pushing across ID on Sunday continues pushing \neast-northeast across the northern Rockies. The associated upper \ndisturbance will help spark increased convection across the forecast \narea Monday afternoon and evening, while at the same time pushing a \nweak cold front south. This weak front will reach southern Colorado \nsome time Tuesday morning, while at the same time another stronger \npiece of the upper system moves across Montana. This stronger lobe \nof energy is what will affect the region on Wednesday. On both days, \nthe upper ridge to the east begins to flatten and creep west. With \nall that said, plan on enhanced convection chances both days, with \nbest probabilities tied to the higher terrain. Monday will be a hot \nday, most likely the hottest day of the extended period, with highs \nin the 80s for the high valleys, and 90s to near 100F for the plains.\nTuesday will be similar, though perhaps 3 to 4 degrees cooler across \nthe plains due to the weak cold front.\n\nWednesday and Thursday...A stronger cold front drops south across CO \non Wednesday, while flat high pressure builds to the west and sets \nup over southern CA and the Desert SW. High temps both days across \nthe plains will be around 5 to 10 degrees cooler than previous days, \nbringing them much closer to seasonal normals. However, the high \nvalleys are forecast to remain very warm and above normal. Increased \nprecip chances, both in coverage and in QPF amounts, are anticipated \nlate Tue night through Wed and into Thu, and this time frame will \nhave the greatest potential for flash flooding on area burn \nscars.\n\nFriday and Saturday...Long range models want to keep flat high \npressure in place over the Desert SW, allowing brisk westerly flow \naloft on Fri transition to NW flow aloft on Sat as another upper \ndisturbance drops down across the Rocky Mt region and brushes across \nCO to the northeast. The NBM increases max temps both days back to \nabove normal, while indicating some questionable precip chances \nacross the higher terrain. Stay tuned as these out periods will \nlikely go through multiple changes. Moore",
